4 "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's consider each of the given relations.\n\n- Relation I is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive because ${2}^{2}+{2}^{2}≰4.$\n\n- Relation II is an equivalence relation since it's reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.\n\n- Relation III is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive because $\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\notin N.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139534430 17592186045417 true] [17592186421414 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421388 13194139534430 17592186045417 true] [17592186421414 1069 "q-214246" 13194139534430 17592186045417 true] [17592186421414 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534430 17592186045417 true] [17592186421414 1071 "Which of the following relations are equivalence relations on $N?$\n\n- $a\\,R\\,b$ if $a-b$ is a multiple of $2$\n- $a∣b$\n- $a\\,R\\,b$ if $3∣(a+b)$" 13194139534430 17592186045417 true] [17592186421414 1072 #uuid "a3f6ced1-e3e7-51b2-bdb3-c0561413aee0" 13194139534430 17592186045417 true] [17592186421414 1074 "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's consider each of the given relations.\n\n- Relation I is an equivalence relation. \n\n- It is reflexive because for all $a\\in N,$ we have that $a-a=0$ is a multiple of $2.$\n\n- It is symmetric because for all $a,b\\in N,$ if $a-b$ is a multiple of $2,$ then $b-a$ is also a multiple of $2.$\n\n- It is transitive because for all $a,b,c\\in N,$ if $a-b$ is a multiple of $2$ and $b-c$ is a multiple of $2,$ then\n\n$$\na-c=(a-b)+(b-c)\n$$\n\nis also a multiple of $2.$\n\n- Relation II is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not symmetric since $3∣6$ but $6∤3.$\n\n- Relation III is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive since $3∤(1+1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139534430 17592186045417 true] [17592186421415 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421385 13194139534430 17592186045417 true] [17592186421415 1069 "q-214280" 13194139534430 17592186045417 true] [17592186421415 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534430 17592186045417 true] [17592186421415 1071 "Consider the relation $R$ on the set $A={\\alpha ,\\beta }$ defined as \n\n$$\nR={(\\alpha ,\\alpha ),(\\alpha ,\\beta ),(\\beta ,\\beta )}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $R$ is reflexive\n- $R$ is symmetric\n- $R$ is transitive\n- $R$ is an equivalence relation" 13194139534430 17592186045417 true] [17592186421415 1072 #uuid "ae54ed26-0094-5420-99d1-1b8455c5d0e3" 13194139534430 17592186045417 true] [17592186421415 1074 "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nLet's consider each of the properties in turn.\n\n- The relation $R$ is reflexive because $(\\alpha ,\\alpha ),(\\beta ,\\beta )\\in R.$$\\,\\,✓$\n\n- The relation $R$ is *not* symmetric because $(\\alpha ,\\beta )\\in R$ but $(\\beta ,\\alpha )\\notin R.$\n\n- The relation $R$ is transitive because it contains no \"linked\" pairs such that $(x,y)\\in R$ and $(y,z)\\in R.\\,✓$\n\n- The relation is *not* an equivalence relation since it's not symmetric.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534430 17592186045417 true] [17592186421416 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421386 13194139534430 17592186045417 true] [17592186421416 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421392 13194139534430 17592186045417 true] [17592186421416 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421393 13194139534430 17592186045417 true] [17592186421416 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421398 13194139534430 17592186045417 true] [17592186421416 1069 "q-214254" 13194139534430 17592186045417 true] [17592186421416 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534430 17592186045417 true] [17592186421416 1071 "Consider the relation $X⊆Y$ defined on the set $P(Q).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n| The relation is reflexive | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- |\n| The relation is symmetric | {{field-2}} |\n| The relation  is transitive | {{field-3}} |\n| The relation is an equivalence relation | {{field-4}} |" 13194139534430 17592186045417 true] [17592186421416 1072 #uuid "bb9f71b9-54fb-5914-9956-fcba4d5311e6" 13194139534430 17592186045417 true] [17592186421416 1074 "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nLet's consider each of the properties in turn.\n\n- It's $\\text{true}$ that the relation is reflexive. For all $X\\in P(Q),$ $X\\,⊆\\,X.$\n\n- It's $\\text{false}$ that the relation is symmetric. For example, $N⊆Z$ but $Z⊈N.$\n\n- It's $\\text{true}$ that the relation is transitive. For all $X,Y,Z\\in P(Q),$ if $X⊆Y$ and $Y⊆Z,$ then $X⊆Z.$\n\n- I